  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "Heightened declamatory stage narration of 19th-century British melodrama and pantomime, the dominant popular theatre of Victorian London houses such as the Adelphi and Drury Lane. The form takes its name from melos plus drama: spoken text delivered over and between orchestral incidental-music cues, with 'melos' stings underscoring villain entrances, agitatos for chases, and tremolando strings beneath emotional speeches, so the narrator-actor's projected, rhetorically exaggerated delivery is timed against the pit. Dion Boucicault's plays and Henry Irving's celebrated vehicle The Bells (1871) exemplify the style, and the earliest sound recordings of veteran Victorian-trained actors preserve its swooping, oratorical cadence.",
